WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.320
My name is Jess Holtz. I'm a technician&nbsp;
at Boston University, but also an artist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.600
I've been doing these micro-landscapes for a&nbsp;
while now, and these are microsculptures. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.800
made out of insects and plants. And you can see&nbsp;
there's a flower and a fern in front of this one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.960
And my imaging technology of choice&nbsp;
is the Scanning Electron Microscope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.560
but I tilt the stage of the electron&nbsp;
microscope such that you're seeing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.000
kind of straight on instead of looking&nbsp;
from the top. So this is what they look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
like before they go into the SEM. And this&nbsp;
is the one we're going to look at today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.240
a bunch of mostly plants but a few insect parts.&nbsp;
I have loaded it into this instrument, Hitachi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.360
desktop SEM. Take a good look at it, because&nbsp;
it's going to look very different on the SEM.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.160
Okay so what we're starting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:17.760
is a hydrangea flower that has been sort&nbsp;
of eaten and we can zoom in quite a bit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:30.160
We're at 25 times magnification. Let's start&nbsp;
moving around. Down here we've got a leaf and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:38.000
covered with these trichromes which is like, if&nbsp;
you see the fuzz on a leaf this is what the fuzz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.680
looks like, although it can look sometimes they're&nbsp;
like branched sometimes they're very different.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:53.840
Okay so this is a bug, obviously.&nbsp;
We can look at the compound eyes.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.880
So yeah, insect eyes are covered with these&nbsp;
ommatidia that are little lens lids that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:08.960
give you to the image being formed. Down here we&nbsp;
have an insect leg covered in these sensory hairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.880
kind of like whiskers. Keep moving.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:22.080
This is a plant, I'm not sure&nbsp;
what type but it's pretty cool.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:28.160
These really interesting little like&nbsp;
tentacle-like things most likely trichromes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.840
Keep moving. Another leaf covered in trichomes.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.760
It's kind of shriveled, and there's&nbsp;
some pollen coming out here.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:47.760
I am very drawn by the detail, just&nbsp;
like anyone. It really gets me. But for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:54.400
making an image for art, I try to actually&nbsp;
make them into landscapes. You know, I'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.240
searching. I try to get something sort of with the&nbsp;
foreground, middle ground, background. I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:06.400
want sort of the illusion of the landscape this&nbsp;
landscape is like maybe half a centimeter across.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:19.680
My specialty is light microscopy, so to&nbsp;
kind of give you orientation...so this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:25.760
this is a commercial, what we call turnkey&nbsp;
confocal microscope. So it has a research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.960
grade with inverted fluorescence microscope&nbsp;
down at the bottom and attached to that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.840
a confocal scan head. And then on the floor that&nbsp;
you can't see is a box that has a bunch of lasers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:43.760
that allow us to excite the fluorescence&nbsp;
in our specimens. So what we are looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:51.040
is pollen that I gathered from the rhododendron&nbsp;
bush out front. So as I mentioned, so the confocal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:57.440
microscope is quite a sophisticated instrument.&nbsp;
In this situation, we have control over what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:04.160
parts of the microscope are being used, what&nbsp;
colors of light we use to illuminate the sample,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.400
as well as what colors of light we are detecting,&nbsp;
right? And those are going to be specific to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:16.160
fluorescent molecules that we are working with.&nbsp;
So here we have like a pile of these pollen grains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:24.080
and we're imaging this with a 20x objective. This&nbsp;
entire field of view represents 320 micrometers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.840
so one third of a millimeter, right. So&nbsp;
this is probably close to 200x resolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.400
So as I adjust the focus of the microscope,&nbsp;
so we're moving the objective lens relative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.840
to the sample, we can start to see that there's&nbsp;
a very specific structure to these pollen grains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.640
right? Some of them have kind of a circle, some&nbsp;
of them have this kind of triangular shape right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:51.920
and so we can see here right so this guy in&nbsp;
the middle is probably kind of dehydrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:58.160
because he's really small, right? This guy has&nbsp;
a nice round one and then these are deflated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:05.280
I'm not a plant biologist so I don't know&nbsp;
exactly what that means. But right, we can adjust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.280
how much light we put into the system. So&nbsp;
if we illuminate with lower intensity light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:18.000
we get a darker image and so in this case the&nbsp;
blue color is coming from the software. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.640
detector is a black and white detector, so as far&nbsp;
as the computer sees. This is a digital image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:29.680
it is a matrix of numbers, right. And then the&nbsp;
computer is really nice and gives it an intensity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:35.840
so that our brain can then interpret this&nbsp;
pattern right. Another thing that's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:42.160
I really like about using the microscope is that&nbsp;
understanding the physiology of sight and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.760
neuroscience of sight and pattern recognition&nbsp;
influences your understanding of what you see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:54.320
So we can set up the microscope to generate&nbsp;
a three-dimensional data set, so it can take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:59.360
a single image and then adjust the focus a very&nbsp;
specific amount and kind of step up through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.840
sample. So this is one I took ahead of time and&nbsp;
then we can actually visualize these structures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.720
in three dimensions that we can try to understand&nbsp;
how different structures--right so in this case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:13.840
we can move this guy sideways--we can try to&nbsp;
understand how different structures are organized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:19.840
relative to each other in three-dimensional space.&nbsp;
Perspective is really valuable for interpretation.

